in Frankfurt. Meanwhile, Germany's defense minister says he believes military conscription will not be needed. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Ilse Aigner tipped by party allies as next German president</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4ce0535a581ba0ce86aabddb1136f5ba2609021f402f49f3caf4008be0a5c717</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:57:28Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T18:48:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ilse-aigner-tipped-by-party-allies-as-next-german-president/a-79880594--62497e57d523.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ilse-aigner-tipped-by-party-allies-as-next-german-president/a-79880594?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">German media reports suggest that Bavarian politician Ilse Aigner's chances of becoming the next president are on the up. Long favored by her Bavarian CSU, now it seems Christian Democrats nationwide plan to back her.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany: Small plane crashes onto A2 highway, killing 2</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c0b22df14d1aefb099a25002e68f76c377847b87359234ce48b273bd6a1f0963</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:57:28Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T16:01:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-small-plane-crashes-onto-a2-highway-killing-2/a-79878930--fda2d6a1e11f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-small-plane-crashes-onto-a2-highway-killing-2/a-79878930?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Two people have died after a small plane crashed onto Germany's A2 highway near Magdeburg. The crash closed the Hannover-bound lanes on a major east-west route, with no road vehicles reported to be involved.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Trial of alleged 'Reichsbürger' group ringleaders begins</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5dfa97de1486242c0d85e4c2da5d2016e3f79535185d4011faf4bc052b0ba593</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:57:28Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T08:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-trial-of-alleged-reichsb_rger-group-ringleaders-begins/live-79872046--a2b502d3bfa3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-trial-of-alleged-reichsbürger-group-ringleaders-begins/live-79872046?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Four members of the banned far-right "Kingdom of Germany" are on trial for belonging to a criminal organization. Meanwhile, a new study warns data centers could lead to water stress in Germany.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany hopes to host world ice hockey championship — without Russia</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:636e0204ceb59d7ffb5b7b983455e1fb51d65ef19ea2fd2ef4e5c25841d167d6</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:58:37Z</updated><published>2026-11-07T11:06:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-hopes-to-host-world-ice-hockey-championship-without-russia/a-79879536--60661c53ebb2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-hopes-to-host-world-ice-hockey-championship-without-russia/a-79879536?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">International protests continue after a court ruled that Russia must be allowed to return to international ice hockey. Germany is seeking a solution that will allow the country to host next year's world championship.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Germany’s Gen Z protests spark debate on integration, bias</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c2b64639f327faa82a7d3a134ea3a01abf59b89f3177c7f3e9ce87297173c3d6</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:57:28Z</updated><published>2026-11-05T16:31:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-gen-z-protests-spark-debate-on-integration-bias/a-79868497--c2e5ae27f840.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-gen-z-protests-spark-debate-on-integration-bias/a-79868497?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Political debate about the recent Gen Z riots have raised the question: Can Germany talk about problems with integration without putting entire groups of people under suspicion?</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>Pioneering Jewish academy opens in Germany</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:65e9f30b18582d4e6b35821b88bf125fdecf75122ece581cb3716dff10073b6b</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:57:28Z</updated><published>2026-11-05T11:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/pioneering-jewish-academy-opens-in-germany/a-79863293--10bb65c14f4e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/pioneering-jewish-academy-opens-in-germany/a-79863293?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A Jewish academy has opened its doors in Frankfurt. The Central Council of Jews in Germany, the country’s leading Jewish umbrella group, set up the project to combat hate and promote dialogue and learning nationally.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>The 2011 discovery of neo-Nazi killers aired biases in German policing</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:185f98344238dfde3289af4e6037dfe2891eb4db881ac7bdca1be3bf791d2040</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:57:28Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T17:16:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-2011-discovery-of-neo-nazi-killers-aired-biases-in-german-policing/a-79857781--949d4ea66003.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-2011-discovery-of-neo-nazi-killers-aired-biases-in-german-policing/a-79857781?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Many questions still surround the National Socialist Underground whose existence was revealed by neo-Nazi terrorist Beate Zschäpe on November 4, 2011. How has German society reacted to NSU crimes? Has justice been done?</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>The dachshund, a humorous icon with political symbolism</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fcad094f4a9f7f57f486e175adf4a7fddc27e16b2628019f32c6322923422652</id><updated>2026-11-07T14:57:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-30T07:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191--0ec0144d606e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">From Picasso to Andy Warhol, a surprising number of artists have portrayed the German breed. But the dachshund has also at times been co-opted as a political symbol.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry></feed>
